AVM FRITZ!OS kernel - README
============================

AVM FRITZ!OS kernel is a Linux derivate.  If you are familiar with building
Linux kernels, take a look at linux/.config.avm, and go ahead with the
source-tree in linux/.  For those, who like to get some initial guidance for
building the FRITZ!OS kernel, here are some hints.

 * To get more reproducible build output, you might want to enable the settings
   for SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH, KBUILD_BUILD_USER,
   KBUILD_BUILD_HOST and KBUILD_BUILD_TIMESTAMP in linux/.kernelvariables by
   setting 'reproducibility' to a nonempty value.

 * Adjust the toolchain variables, to match your cross toolchain as required:
   CROSS_COMPILE (e.g.  mips-linux-gnu-), CC (e.g. mips-linux-gnu-gcc-5),
   HOSTCC (e.g. gcc).  You can either update linux/.kernelvariables, or
   override the AVM defaults via command line options, e.g.

    make ARCH=riscv CC=riscv-linux-gnu-gcc CROSS_COMPILE=riscv-linux-gnu-

 * For some, the Makefile in this directory might be helpful for first steps
   towards compiling your F!OS kernel.  Install the kernel build dependencies
   (cp. linux/Documentation/process/changes.rst) and run 'make' for some help.

If you want to build your kernel in-source, then:

 * Copy 'linux/.config.avm' to 'linux/.config'

if you want to build your kernel out-of-source, with build results in ${BUILD},
then

 * Copy 'linux/.config.avm' to '${BUILD}/.config'


Example for building the F!OS kernel for a arm64 board out-of-source:

    cd source/kernel
    mkdir build
    cp linux/.config.avm build/.config
    make -C linux O=../build ARCH=arm64 CC=aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu- -j$(nproc)


Note: Running 'make mrproper' in the Linux source tree removes also
linux/.kernelvariables.  You might want to restore it by cloning from
linux/.kernelvariables.gpl.


Thanks for caring for your open-source kernel.

