FAQs
====

Q: Can I use my host distribution's toolchain for compiling the kernel?
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Maybe.  Upstream Linux kernel versions are developed for a specific range of
gcc/clang versions, but FRITZ!OS kernels are developed only with one specific
gcc version, possibly with specific features enabled or disabled (as provided
in the GPL package).  Compiler versions that are too old for the specific Linux
version will not be able to compile the code due to a lack of features or
language extensions in upstream gcc.  Compiler versions that are newer than the
one we provide in the GPL package may be able to build the kernel, but might
also lead to build failures as they tend to be better in detecting bad code and
might error-out.

Thus, even if you supply the original board specific environment variables
(e.g. ARCH), your toolchain might have different features or language
extensions enabled than the toolchain we use at AVM (watch out for CC_HAS_*
kconfig symbols).

To verify, that all important kconfig symbols are unmodified, you might want
run

    linux/scripts/diffconfig linux/.config ${BUILD}/.config

So, if you want to reproduce the FRITZ!OS kernel, please stick to the toolchain
and its configurations as supplied in the GPL archive.  If you experience
kconfig changes or build failures with the original FRITZ!OS kernel
configuration, even though you use the toolchain as delivered via the AVM GPL
package, please contact us (as this might be a serious bug that slipped through
our automated testing).


Q: How is the FRITZ!OS kernel build system different from Linux upstream?
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

FRITZ!OS kernels carry some extensions that need adjustments in kbuild.  We
attempt to keep the kbuild changes in upstream sources as small as possible,
but put several files around that hook into some parts:

1. $(KBUILD_OUTPUT)/.kernelvariables or linux/.kernelvariables

  holds kbuild related environment variables like ARCH, CC, CROSS_COMPILE etc.
  Some variables might need to be adjusted, e.g. if a different toolchain is
  used; variables can be overidden via make command line.  .kernelvariables is
  sourced by GNUmakefile, see below.


2. GNUmakefile

   We ship and use a GNUmakefile to set up several environment variables that
   are needed for FRITZ!OS kernel builds, and we wrap some kbuild targets, e.g.:

     * Source .kernelvariables for cached variables required for kernel builds.

     * Copy avm/conf/${whatever} (see AVM_KCONFIG_CONFIG in .kernelvariables)
       to $(KBUILD_OUTPUT)/.config, if it is newer than
       $(KBUILD_OUTPUT)/.config.

     * Find "LiSI" subtrees (cp. below) and forward their additional include
       paths.

     * Show a kconfig diff between $(KBUILD_OUTPUT)/.config and
       $(AVM_KCONFIG_CONFIG) (if enabled in .kernelvariables).

     * Install in-tree kmods twice: stripped (/lib/modules/...) and unstripped
       (/usr/lib/debug/lib/modules/...).

     * When building external kmods: Install a project specific *.symvers file
       along with the kernel module(s).

3. "Linux Subproject Integration" (LiSI)

   Some AVM specific Linux subtrees contain a avm/conf/ folder, and their
   include folders will be put into the list of Linux in-tree or UAPI header
   directories.  These subtrees are thus providing C header files for other
   kernel code (in-tree as well as external kmods) and for user-space.
